WEBVTT

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.630
A gaming PC becomes a capable AI workstation through deliberate configuration,

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.115
not through a single installation or expensive upgrade.

00:00:07.115 --> 00:00:12.520
Begin with an exact inventory of the GPU, VRAM, CPU, system RAM, storage, motherboard,

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.950
power supply, and cooling.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:17.745
Those facts establish the machine's operating envelope and prevent model or hardware

00:00:17.745 --> 00:00:19.680
choices based on assumptions.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.890
Select an operating system that you can maintain and that supports the intended

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.470
acceleration path.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.969
Treat GPU drivers, CUDA, ROCm, Vulkan, and the selected runtime as a compatibility chain.

00:00:28.969 --> 00:00:32.595
Prove that acceleration is active with a simple setup before adding interfaces and

00:00:32.595 --> 00:00:33.550
orchestration.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.280
Then choose models by workload and available memory.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.895
Quantization, context length, and partial offload are engineering tradeoffs,

00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:42.565
not magic ways to erase resource limits.

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:44.000
Measure real tasks.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.960
Generation speed, prompt processing, GPU utilization, VRAM, system RAM, temperature,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.040
power, and noise together describe the user experience.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.360
Change one variable at a time and preserve a known-good baseline.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.820
Upgrade only when evidence identifies a clear bottleneck and the proposed part can be

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:58.910
supported by the case,

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.350
motherboard, power supply, and cooling system.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.120
Finally, protect the workstation.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.165
Use trusted model and software sources, restrict API exposure,

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:10.715
control sensitive prompts and logs, patch deliberately, and keep configuration backups.

00:01:10.715 --> 00:01:14.505
The goal is not to run the biggest available model or assemble the most complicated stack.

00:01:14.505 --> 00:01:17.770
The goal is reliable, private local AI that serves a defined workload,

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:21.630
remains understandable to its operator, and can be recovered when something changes.
